{"cuisine/belizean-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/belizean-cuisine","n":"Belizean cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Belize","intro":[["Belizean cuisine is an amalgamation of all ethnicities in the nation of Belize and their respectively wide variety of foods. Breakfast often consists of sides of bread, flour tortillas, or fry jacks that are often homemade and eaten with various cheeses. All are often accompanied with refried beans, cheeses, and various forms of eggs. Inclusive is also cereal along with milk, coffee, or tea."],["Midday meals vary, from lighter foods such as rice and beans, tamales, panades (fried meat pies), escabeche (onion soup), chimole/chirmole (soup), stew chicken, garnaches (fried tortillas with beans, cheese, and diced onion sauce or diced cabbage) to various constituted dinners featuring some type of rice and beans, meat and salad or coleslaw."],["In the rural areas meals may be more simplified than in the cities. The Maya use recado, corn or maize for most of their meals, and the Garifuna are fond of seafood, cassava (particularly made into cassava bread or ereba) and vegetables. Local fruits are quite common, raw vegetables from the markets less so. Mealtime is a communion for families and schools and some businesses close at midday for lunch, reopening later in the afternoon."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"belizean-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belizean_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Taking Belize: Belizean Recipes","ref":"site/takingbelize-blogspot-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"takingbelize.blogspot.com","note":"Collection of Belizean home recipes on a personal blog.","url":"https://takingbelize.blogspot.com/p/belizean-recipes.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/58/Fry_jacks_with_eggs_and_salsa.jpg/500px-Fry_jacks_with_eggs_and_salsa.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"US Embassy Belize","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fry_jacks_with_eggs_and_salsa.jpg"}},"cuisine/bahraini-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/bahraini-cuisine","n":"Bahraini cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Eastern Arabian cuisine","cuisine/eastern-arabian-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Bahrain","intro":[["The cuisine of Bahrain consists of dishes such as biryani, harees, khabeesa, machboos, mahyawa, quzi and zalabia. Arabic coffee (qahwah) is the national beverage."],["Bahrain is a small island state near the western shores of the Persian Gulf. Much of the cuisine of Bahrain is a mixture of Arabic, Iranian (national, Balochi, and Achomi cuisine), Indian, African, Far East and European food due to the influence of the various communities present, as Bahrain has been an important seaport and trading junction since ancient times."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"bahraini-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bahraini_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Odehlicious (Khaleeji)","ref":"site/theodehlicious-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"theodehlicious.com","note":"Khaleeji section of a Middle Eastern home cooking blog.","url":"https://theodehlicious.com/cuisines/khaleeji/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Cardamom and Lime: Recipes from the Arabian Gulf","ref":"cookbook/cardamom-and-lime-recipes-from-the-arabian-gulf","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Sarah al-Hamad","note":"Home cooking from the Gulf states as a whole, not this country alone.","url":"https://www.simonandschuster.ca/books/Cardamom-and-Lime/Sarah-al-Hamad/9781566567251","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781566567251"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Eastern Arabian cuisine","cuisine/eastern-arabian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3c/Manama_Abendessen_04.jpg/500px-Manama_Abendessen_04.jpg","w":500,"h":371,"by":"Zairon","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manama_Abendessen_04.jpg"}},"cuisine/apulian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/apulian-cuisine","n":"Pugliese cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Italy","intro":[["Apulian cuisine is characterized above all by the importance given to raw materials, both land and sea, and by the fact that all the ingredients are designed to enhance and not alter the basic flavours of the products used."],["Therefore, it has a large variety of seasonal vegetables, legumes and seafood. Moreover, even if there are some common dishes, the recipes vary from province to province and, sometimes, from city to city: for example, the typical recipes of the provinces of Taranto, Brindisi and Bari, located on the sea, are not the same as those practiced in the province of Foggia, which is more hilly, and that of Lecce, which is more inland."],["There are many recipes in this cuisine, which has a particularity that distinguishes it from others, that of offering different dishes in relation to the different seasons, so that during the milder seasons, that is in spring and summer, preference is given to vegetables, fish and seafood, while in the other seasons legumes predominate, as well as home-made pasta seasoned with various sauces, alone or combined with vegetables or fish."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"apulian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apulian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine"},"cuisine/kanaka-maoli-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/kanaka-maoli-foodways","n":"Kānaka Maoli foodways","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Oceania","cuisines#oceania"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Hawaiian Islands","names":"Kānaka Maoli, Native Hawaiians","intro":[["Kalo, or ",["Taro","ingredient/taro"],", is honored as an elder brother. It grows in loʻi, terraces flooded with water led from streams, and pounded and fermented into ",["Poi","dish/poi-hawaiian-food"],", it's part of almost every traditional Hawaiian meal. People show it respect: no harsh words while planting or eating it, and the poi bowl is covered if business comes up at the table."],["Food traditionally came from the ahupuaʻa, land divisions running from the mountains to the sea. Upland forests gave ʻulu, ",["Breadfruit","ingredient/breadfruit"],", and gardens gave ʻuala (",["Sweet potato","ingredient/sweet-potato"],"), sugarcane and maiʻa (banana), while stone-walled loko iʻa, fishponds, raised fish along the shore, fed partly with kalo peels. Community groups are restoring loʻi kalo and fishponds on Maui, Molokaʻi and Oʻahu, and growers are planting far more ʻulu."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"National Museum of the American Indian, American Indian magazine, The breath of life","url":"https://www.americanindianmagazine.org/story/the-breath-of-life"},{"name":"Ka Wai Ola (Office of Hawaiian Affairs), Mālama ʻāina and food sovereignty in Maui Nui","url":"https://kawaiola.news/aina/malama-aina-and-food-sovereignty-in-maui-nui/"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"kanaka-maoli-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Poi (Hawaiian food)","dish/poi-hawaiian-food"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Swamp taro","ingredient/swamp-taro"],["Taro","ingredient/taro"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"Oceanic cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/05/Bowl_of_poi.jpg/500px-Bowl_of_poi.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Bshams","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bowl_of_poi.jpg","of":"Poi (Hawaiian food)"}},"ingredient/rolled-oats":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/rolled-oats","n":"Rolled oats","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Rolled oats are a type of lightly processed whole-grain food. They are made from oat groats that have been dehusked and steamed, before being rolled into flat flakes under heavy rollers and then stabilized by being lightly toasted."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-rolled-oats"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rolled_oats"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["Australian cuisine","cuisine/australian-cuisine",[["Anzac biscuit","dish/anzac-biscuit"]]],["New Zealand cuisine","cuisine/new-zealand-cuisine",[["Anzac biscuit","dish/anzac-biscuit"]]],["Scottish cuisine","cuisine/scottish-cuisine",[["Hobnob biscuit","dish/hobnob-biscuit"]]],["Swedish cuisine","cuisine/swedish-cuisine",[["Oatmeal ball","dish/oatmeal-ball"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Golden syrup","ingredient/golden-syrup"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Cocoa powder","ingredient/cocoa-powder"],["Coffee","ingredient/coffee"],["Grated coconut","ingredient/grated-coconut"],["Oatmeal","ingredient/oatmeal"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/97/Rolled_oats.jpg/500px-Rolled_oats.jpg","w":500,"h":324,"by":"en:user:Hankwang","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rolled_oats.jpg"}},"ingredient/whipped-cream":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/whipped-cream","n":"Whipped cream","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 12 dishes across 14 cuisines","intro":[["Whipped cream, also known as Chantilly cream or crème Chantilly, is high-fat dairy cream, known as heavy cream, whipping cream or double cream, that is aerated by whisking until it becomes light, fluffy, and capable of holding its shape. This process incorporates air into the cream, creating a semi-solid colloid. It is commonly sweetened with white sugar and often flavored with vanilla. Whipped cream is often served on desserts and hot beverages and used as an ingredient in desserts."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-whipped-cream"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whipped_cream"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":14,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Icebox cake","dish/icebox-cake"],["Strawberry delight","dish/strawberry-delight"]]],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Mont Blanc (dessert)","dish/mont-blanc-dessert"],["Profiterole","dish/profiterole"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Mont Blanc (dessert)","dish/mont-blanc-dessert"],["Profiterole","dish/profiterole"]]],["Swedish cuisine","cuisine/swedish-cuisine",[["Flying Jacob","dish/flying-jacob"],["Semla","dish/semla"]]],["Danish cuisine","cuisine/danish-cuisine",[["Risalamande","dish/risalamande"]]],["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine",[["Mango float","dish/mango-float"]]],["Finnish cuisine","cuisine/finnish-cuisine",[["Semla","dish/semla"]]],["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine",[["Spaghettieis","dish/spaghettieis"]]],["Mindanao cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-mindanao",[["Mango float","dish/mango-float"]]],["Norwegian cuisine","cuisine/norwegian-cuisine",[["Risalamande","dish/risalamande"]]],["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Serradura","dish/serradura"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Nesselrode pudding","dish/nesselrode-pudding"]]],["Thuringian cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine-thuringia",[["Spaghettieis","dish/spa